DevOps Journey – EC2 Tools Installation

Objective

Hey, I’m Kaustubh 👋
I’m a Java developer and I’ve just started my DevOps journey.

Till now I was only writing code, but I never really understood how things run on servers and in cloud. So I decided to start from basics. In this task, I created my own AWS EC2 server and installed important DevOps tools step by step.

This is just the beginning, but I’m learning slowly and building my foundation properly 🚀

EC2 Instance Details

Cloud Provider: AWS
Service: EC2 (Elastic Compute Cloud)
Operating System: Amazon Linux
Instance Type: t3.micro (Free tier)
Storage: 8 GB gp3
Login Method: EC2 Browser Connect

This instance is acting like my personal DevOps lab server.

Tools Installed in EC2

Python Installation

First I installed Python because many tools and automation scripts depend on Python.

Commands used:
sudo yum install python -y
python3 --version

After installation my version was Python 3.9.25.

AWS CLI Installation

AWS CLI is very important because it allows us to control AWS services directly from terminal instead of using AWS Console.

Commands used:
curl "https://awscli.amazonaws.com/awscli-exe-linux-x86_64.zip
" -o "awscliv2.zip"
sudo yum install unzip -y
unzip awscliv2.zip
sudo ./aws/install
aws --version

After installing, my AWS CLI version was 2.33.19.

Git Installation

Git is required for version control and cloning repositories from GitHub.

Command used:
sudo yum install git -y
git --version

Now git is installed and ready to clone repos.

Java Installation

Since most enterprise applications are Java based (like Spring Boot and Jenkins), Java is required.

Command used:
sudo yum install java -y
java --version

Java installed successfully.

Maven Installation

Maven is used to build and package Java projects.

Commands used:
cd /opt
wget apache maven tar file
tar extract command
Created maven.sh file inside /etc/profile.d
Added MAVEN_HOME and PATH variables
Loaded the file using source command
Checked version using mvn -v

Maven installed successfully.

Jenkins Installation

Jenkins is a CI/CD automation tool which builds, tests and deploys application automatically.

Commands used:
sudo yum update -y
Added Jenkins repo
Imported Jenkins key
Installed Jenkins
Started Jenkins service
Enabled Jenkins service
Checked status

Jenkins service is running.

Docker Installation

Docker is used to containerize applications so they can run anywhere without environment issues.

Commands used:
sudo yum install docker -y
Started docker service
Enabled docker
Added jenkins user to docker group
Checked docker version

Docker installed successfully.